$\mathrm{N}_2(g)+3 \mathrm{H}_2(g) \rightleftharpoons 2 \mathrm{NH}_3(g)\begin{array}{ll}1 \text { mole } 3 \text { mole } &amp; 0 \text { moles (2 L) (initially) } \\ 0.6 \text { or } \quad(3-3 x) &amp; 2 x \text { moles } \\ (1-x) \text { moles moles } &amp; \text { (at equilibrium) }\end{array}
Number of moles of $\mathrm{N}_2=0.6=1-x\therefore x=1-0.6=0.4$
So, $3-3 x=3-3 \times 0.4=1.8$,
and $2 x=2 \times 0.4=0.8$
Therefore, the total number of moles at equilibrium.
$=(1+x)+(3-3 x)+2 x=0.6+1.8+0.8=3.2 \mathrm{~mol}
